Every construct here stands INSIDE a sentence, the way a reader meets one. A construct read alone
answers a different question than the same construct read mid-paragraph: alone it needs only to
carry its own scopes, and in prose it must also let a reader's eye LAND on it.

Measured across sixty-five bundled themes, the opening glyph of every bracketed construct below
paints at a perceptual distance of 0.0 from the prose beside it in forty of them. Themes rule on
`punctuation` in order to say LEAVE IT ALONE, and Bringhurst §3.5.2 agrees: the words, not the
punctuation, merit emphasis. So a construct lands through its CONTENT or it does not land.

! The bracket family, in prose

The note at {{MyTiddler}} explains the rest of it plainly.

The note at {{MyTiddler||$:/core/ui/TagTemplate}} explains the rest of it plainly.

The note at {{{ [tag[Done]sort[title]] }}} explains the rest of it plainly.

The note at [[Link Text|TiddlerTitle]] explains the rest of it plainly.

The note at [ext[caption|https://example.com]] explains the rest of it plainly.

The note at [img[alt text|pic.png]] explains the rest of it plainly.

The note at <<greeting name="world" tone="warm">> explains the rest of it plainly.

The note at <$list filter="[tag[x]]" variable="i"/> explains the rest of it plainly.

! The controls

A code span lands in fifty-three of sixty-five themes, where every neighbour above lands in
twenty-five. It reaches a real theme rule at its FIRST character, and it stands as the only
construct in this grammar that does.

The note at `code span` explains the rest of it plainly.

The note at ''bold text'' explains the rest of it plainly.

! Two pairs a reader needs apart

A called thing against its argument reads as one colour in thirty-nine of sixty-five themes,
`variable.name.macro` and `variable.parameter` both reaching a theme's bare `variable` rule.

<<greeting name="world">>

A widget against its attribute reads as one colour in twenty of sixty-five, `entity.name.function`
and `entity.other.attribute-name` both reaching a bare `entity` rule.

<$list filter="[tag[Done]]" variable="item"/>

